 Interactive tasks shift the target again. Correctness may concern final state, compliance with a policy, user consent, and the quality of a conversation. A terminal judge can miss an earlier irreversible violation. The more ambiguous the objective and the later the feedback, the less justified it is to import sample-count gains from exact-answer tasks. Extra computation can still help by collecting evidence, checking a plan against explicit constraints, or generating alternatives for human choice. What changes is the basis of confidence: agreement and judge preference must not be described as a certificate of truth.
 
-This leads to five positive choices, elaborated with the calculation and decision tree below. Use more independent samples when correct candidates exist and can be identified. Use deeper revision or search when intermediate feedback is informative and the state can be safely retained or revisited. Invest in verification when coverage outruns selection. Use a stronger base model when the current generator has little useful support or repeated attempts exploit weak tests. Use a simpler path when the marginal expected benefit is below cost, the existing solution already meets the requirement, or additional computation has no new signal to resolve the remaining uncertainty.
